<section class="shell band faq">
  <h2>Questions about the CEUs</h2>

  <details>
    <summary>How many ethics CEUs do Texas LPCs need?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>At least 6 hours of ethics within the 24 hours of continuing education required each
      renewal period. Passing the jurisprudence exam counts as one of those ethics hours. LPC
      Supervisors need a further 6 hours of supervision-specific CE, counted inside the same
      24.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Do these hours count toward the approved-provider half?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Yes. Since 1 January 2024 at least half of your 24 hours must come from a provider
      listed in 22 TAC §681.140(f), and subsection (f)(6) lists <strong>a licensee with
      supervisor status</strong>. Reem holds LPC Supervisor status in Texas, license
      {{ site.license }}, so these hours fill that half rather than only the free half.</p>
      <p>There is no approval number to look up, for this or any other Texas CE: under
      §681.140(i) the Council does not pre-approve providers or individual courses. The
      licensee stays responsible for their own documentation in an audit, so keep the
      certificate.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Is the online session self-study?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Worth asking of any online CE, including this one. Under §681.140(h) self-study is
      capped at <strong>one hour per renewal period</strong>, and a recorded course counts as
      self-study while a live session does not. Ask whether an online course is delivered live
      or as a recording before you book, because the answer decides whether a second recorded
      hour is worth anything to you.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>What does the trail walk session involve physically?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Walking at conversation pace on a trail, with time standing among horses. It is not
      strenuous and there is no riding, but it is outdoors in central Texas, so it is weather
      dependent and gets rescheduled rather than pushed through.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Can LPC Associates attend?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Yes. Associates carry the same continuing education requirements as fully licensed LPCs
      and are welcome at both formats. Associates supervised by Reem are included in the annual
      retreat as part of their supervision.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

